{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a6fad1e9e6a67cec", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tComparing British and American Audio Description of Movies\n2\tTitle:\n3\tContent selection saved. Describe the issue below:\n4\tDescription:\n5\tarXiv is now an independent nonprofit!\n6\tLearn more\n7\t×\n8\tLicense: CC BY 4.0\n9\tarXiv:2608.09792v1 [cs.CL] 10 Aug 2026\n10\t\\copyrightclause\n11\tCopyright for this paper by its authors.\n12\tUse permitted under Creative Commons License Attribution 4.0\n13\tInternational (CC BY 4.0).\n14\t\\conference\n15\tCMN’26: Workshop on Computational Models of Narrative, June 8-10, 2026, Madrid, Spain.\n16\t[email=igor.sterner@ed.ac.uk,\n17\t]\n18\t[email=alex@inf.ed.ac.uk,\n19\t]\n20\t[email=keller@inf.ed.ac.uk,\n21\t]\n22\tComparing British and American Audio Description of Movies\n23\tIgor Sterner\n24\tSchool of Informatics, University of Edinburgh, United Kingdom\n25\tAlex Lascarides\n26\tFrank Keller\n27\t(2026)\n28\tAbstract\n29\tNarrating the visual component of movies is known as audio description. It is a narrative technique designed to enable blind and visually impaired individuals to follow the story. However, it is far more constrained than most narratives: the descriptions not only need to convey the story in the movie, but they must also fit into gaps between dialogue and they need to conform to guidelines that exist in each region. In this work, we compare audio description created in the United Kingdom against audio description created in the United States. We use guidelines written for these two regions, alongside the impressions from a practitioner in the field, to motivate specific hypotheses about the differences. We test these hypotheses against our pre-existing corpus, which provides both human-authored American and British audio description for each of 206 movies. Results provide quantitative evidence to uphold all tested hypotheses, including differences in lexicon, the use of the progressive aspect, the use of passive constructions, the use of subjective adjectives and modifiers, when characters are named, how scenes are cued, and degree of overlap with movie dialogue and music. Our work offers a quantitative lens into the narrative technique of audio description.\n30\tkeywords:\n31\tmovies\n32\t\\sep\n33\taudio description\n34\t\\sep\n35\tguidelines\n36\t\\sep\n37\tnarrative\n38\t\\sep\n39\tdialect\n40\t1\n41\tIntroduction\n42\tMovies tell stories using a carefully curated mix of linguistic, auditory and visual information.\n43\tAudiences extract meaning and narrative from all of this information, often to great enjoyment.\n44\tHowever, being able to articulate that meaning-making process is a challenge.\n45\tThis is a challenge tackled by accessibility services that cater to individuals for whom one or more modality is less available.\n46\tThe accessibility service that aims to cater to blind and visually impaired individuals is called audio description (AD).\n47\tAD is a translation of the visual (and unclear aural) information into a verbal narration.\n48\tThe narration is constrained to fit in gaps between dialogue, and it is voiced by a neutral narrator.\n49\tThe task requires an audio describer to begin with a full understanding of the narrative being told\n50\t[\n51\tvercauteren-2016-narratological\n52\t]\n53\t, which involves analysis of the full movie and potentially also a screenplay, dialogue subtitles, and the other available resources.\n54\tThey must then identify suitable gaps between dialogue and find ways to translate the visual part of the narrative into a verbal narrative.\n55\tEvery AD will be different, because these stages involve subjective interpretation and storytelling.\n56\tAD is arguably a literary and narrative art form\n57\t[\n58\tsnyder-2008-audio\n59\t]\n60\t.\n61\tThere is debate about how much creative license should be afforded to audio describers.\n62\tThis debate spans a scale of strictly objective description of the visuals\n63\t[\n64\tadc_2009_guidelines\n65\t]\n66\tto a full re-narrativisation of the movie that prioritises coherent storytelling\n67\t[\n68\tkruger-2010-audio_narration\n69\t]\n70\t.\n71\tFor practical purposes, some parts of this debate are answered for particular regions by their AD guidelines.\n72\tFor instance, American guidelines\n73\t[Annexe 4 of\n74\trai-2010-comparative\n75\t]\n76\tstipulate that all subjective interpretation is unacceptable, while British guidelines\n77\t[Annexe 6 of\n78\trai-2010-comparative\n79\t]\n80\tstate that when it reduces potential confusion, particular visuals can be subject to a certain amount of interpretation.\n81\tUltimately, however, all perception is interpretive and complete objectivity is simply impossible.\n82\tAudio describers rely on their training, experience, and common sense in order to maximise the understanding and enjoyment (i.e., access) that they provide to their audience.\n83\tGiven the overarching goal of providing AD is achieving access, the extent to which American and British AD scripts differ in practice has never been quantitatively evaluated.\n84\tConsider the AD of the Trinity nuclear test scene in the 2023 movie Oppenheimer.\n85\t1\n86\t1\n87\t1\n88\tThe video of this scene with American AD is available here:\n89\thttps://adawardsgala.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/Oppenheimer.mp4\n90\t.\n91\t\\ex\n92\t. \n93\tK̇istiakowsky is open mouthed, staring through his goggles. In the car, Feynman screws up his eyes from the light. Oppenheimer takes off his goggles. An immense fireball billows into the night sky. Oppenheimer’s eyelids flicker open. The explosion climbs higher and higher, white heat at its core darkening at the edges into an orange mushroom shape. Lawrence is standing outside the car with his hand on the door, watching the spreading glow on the horizon. Oppenheimer stares into the blackened orange clouds with sparks flying in their midst. On his mattress, Rabi shifts onto his side for a clearer view, keeping the tinted glass in front of his face. Other observers sit up to watch the still-blossoming explosion. Rabi starts to lower his glass. Oppenheimer watches the light fade to a red glimmer.  [UK] \n94\t.̱ Now, standing outside the car, Lawrence gapes as the bright line shines on his face, and Feynman squeezes his eyes shut. Teller’s lips puff out with a sharp exhale as his expression remains unchanged. In the bunker, Oppenheimer removes his goggles, blinking rapidly and squinting to see the explosion growing into a column of fire that blooms high into the dark sky, taking on a mushroom shape. Seen from over Lawrence’s shoulder, the explosion’s brilliant light gives off an amber halo around its irregular edges, which, miles away, tinges Oppenheimer’s awed face. The roiling flames billow over each other, black smoke starting to form within the inferno. Rabi finally rolls over and peers through his welder’s glass at the explosion. The amber glow on Oppenheimer’s face dims as the black smoke grows in the flames, which fold over on themselves. Rabi lowers the glass from his eyes as the blackness consumes the remaining flames, leaving behind a ghostly cloud of smoke in the dark.  [US]\n95\tBoth the British and American versions (\n96\t1\n97\tand\n98\t1\n99\t, respectively) describe similar events in the scene, using language that is largely an objective description of the visuals.\n100\tNevertheless, the language is almost poetic and paints a vivid scene.\n101\tThere is some flexibility, especially in a short thread, as to when an action is described—e.g., compare the description of Lawrence outside a car watching the explosion (7th sentence in UK, 1st sentence in US).\n102\tThere is also some flexibility on which of the less salient actions to describe, as we can see from the choices to describe only one of Kistiakowsky’s or Teller’s expressions.\n103\tA part of the process of creating AD also relies on knowledge of who the audience will be.\n104\tAD created in the United States will follow guidelines from that region; the same holds for AD created in other regions, in particular the United Kingdom.\n105\tIn our study, we will only consider English AD from these two regions, since they form the largest available data, but English AD is also created for other regions (e.g., Australia) and for many other languages.\n106\tEach region will have dialectal and lexical preferences, and audio describers naturally take this into account in their decisions on how to express content.\n107\tComparing the differences between versions of AD can offer a lens into AD as a narrative technique.\n108\tPrior work has relied on theoretical analyses of guidelines\n109\t[\n110\tbittner-2012-guidelines\n111\t]\n112\tand anecdotal evidence\n113\t[\n114\tcampbell-2025-crossing\n115\t]\n116\t.\n117\tThey offer hypotheses as to the differences between American and British AD, but do not provide quantitative evidence in support of these hypotheses.\n118\tIn this paper, we aim to address this gap.\n119\tFor fair and controlled experiments, we need to compare AD of the same source material from each region.\n120\tIn separate work, we have collected a corpus of AD transcripts that makes this possible; see\n121\t[\n122\tsterner-2026-reframed\n123\t]\n124\tfor more details on the corpus and on the automatic generation of AD.\n125\tFor each of 206 movies, the corpus provides a professional American AD transcript and a professional British AD transcript.\n126\tWe will begin by describing hypotheses about the differences between AD in the regions. These are based on lexical variation, aspect, voice, subjectivity, character naming, dialogue overlap and description of scene changes.\n127\tThe specific hypotheses we define are motivated by the AD guidelines and claims made by a leading practitioner in the creation of AD.\n128\tWe design and implement automatic methods that test these hypotheses, and apply them to our corpus.\n129\tResults provide supporting evidence to uphold all of the hypotheses.\n130\tWe finish by discussing the implications of our work.\n131\t2\n132\tHypotheses\n133\tWe make hypotheses of the differences between American and British AD based on two sources.\n134\tThe first source is the AD guidelines from each region.\n135\t2\n136\t2\n137\t2\n138\tRai\n139\t[\n140\trai-2010-comparative\n141\t]\n142\tappends a snapshot of guidelines at the time of their writing to their appendix (the American guidelines we use are in Annexe 4 and British guidelines in Annexe 6).\n143\tAD guidelines provide recommendations as to how audio describers should produce their descriptions.\n144\tOur interpretation of the guidelines and the differences between them is based on Bittner’s analysis\n145\t[\n146\tbittner-2012-guidelines\n147\t]\n148\t.\n149\tThe second source is Campbell’s claims in\n150\t[\n151\tcampbell-2025-crossing\n152\t]\n153\t.\n154\tCampbell works in a leading role in the creation of AD at Red Bee Media, a large broadcasting and media services company.\n155\tHer claims represent the perspective of an AD practitioner.\n156\tFor this section, we will provide examples from one movie, Oppenheimer (2023).\n157\tIts American AD was a finalist in both the EGA Hermes Awards and Audio Description Awards Gala.\n158\tThe first unique aspect of this movie is that the officially released British AD is a post-edit of the American AD, which is uncommon (henceforth, we’ll refer to this version as ‘post-edit’).\n159\t3\n160\t3\n161\t3\n162\tThis is a presumption, based on observing the data and both stating that they are created by the same company, Deluxe. We presume that the American AD version of Oppenheimer was provided as a starting point to a team that scripted and voiced the British AD version.\n163\tThe second unique aspect of this movie is that an entirely new UK AD version was created by the BBC for its broadcasting of the movie (henceforth, we’ll refer to this version as ‘UK’).\n164\tConsider the following scene.\n165\tOppenheimer has been in the New Mexico desert with Lawrence and Frank.\n166\tOppenheimer says ‘Come’, and leads them off.\n167\tWhen the\n168\tscene changes\n169\tto one later in the day in a tent, the AD versions are as follows.\n170\t\\ex\n171\t.\n172\tİnside a tent, Lawrence drinks from a bottle and passes it to Frank.  [UK]\n173\t.̱ After nightfall, warm lantern light glows on the men’s faces inside a tent. Lawrence passes a liquor bottle to Frank, who takes a swig.  [US]\n174\t.̧\n175\tAfter nightfall\n176\tIn a tent at night\n177\t, warm\n178\tlantern\n179\tlight glows on the men’s faces\n180\tinside a tent\n181\t. Lawrence passes a\n182\tliquor\n183\tbottle\n184\tof booze\n185\tto Frank, who takes a swig.  [post-edit]\n186\tAll three versions cue the scene-change with a fronted adverbial (‘inside a tent’; ‘after nightfall’; ‘in a tent at night’).\n187\tBoth British and American AD guidelines state that scene changes should be cued, in particular when it is difficult to infer from the soundtrack.\n188\tThe American guidelines are more explicit in this recommendation, articulating that markers like ‘now’ can be used. In Oppenheimer, ‘now’ is used 16 times in the American version, almost all of which are removed in the post-edit (leaving 3), and also rarely used in the UK version (used 3 times).\n189\tIn contrast, the British guidelines do not provide any explicit suggestions as to how signalling a change in scene should be achieved.\n190\tWe can also see\n191\tlexical variation\n192\tin how the alcoholic beverage is described (UK and post-edit: ‘a bottle’ or ‘a bottle of booze’; US: ‘liquor bottle’).\n193\tThis difference is raised by Campbell, but not by any of the region-specific guidelines.\n194\tConsider now the following description, which occurs at the end of a scene when Oppenheimer is giving a lecture in Holland.\n195\t\\ex\n196\t.\n197\tȮppenheimer is standing at the lectern.  [UK]\n198\t.̱ Oppenheimer stands at a lectern.  [US]\n199\t.̧\n200\tAt the front,\n201\tOppenheimer stands at a lectern\n202\tlooking slightly nervous\n203\t.  [post-edit]\n204\tThe British and American descriptions are near-identical, except for the fact that they differ in\n205\taspect\n206\t.\n207\tBoth are in the present tense, but the British description employs the progressive aspect, while the American description is simple.\n208\tCampbell claims that American descriptions employ almost exclusively the simple present tense, while in British descriptions there is the option to switch to the present progressive and simple present for variation.\n209\tThis claim is supported by the guidelines, which all state that the present tense should be used, with the guidelines from the UK and from Ireland additionally allowing progressive aspect to be used.\n210\tIn the post-edited version of the American description, the describer has also chosen to incorporate a more detailed description of Oppenheimer’s body language (‘looking slightly nervous’).\n211\tNote that the describer has chosen not to describe the visuals directly (e.g. Oppenheimer’s pursed lips or avoidant eyes).\n212\tInstead the adjective ‘nervous’, additionally modified by the adverb ‘slightly’, is used to make a judgment about Oppenheimer’s emotions.\n213\tIn this case, this decision was presumably made due to the very short gap between dialogue into which this description has to fit.\n214\tThe British guidelines explicitly afford flexibility with regard to\n215\tsubjective interpretation\n216\t, in particular when this reduces potential confusion.\n217\tHowever, the American guidelines are explicit in discouraging this.\n218\tCampbell acknowledges the assumption that British AD tends to do more work in interpreting emotions than American AD.\n219\tHowever, she argues that it is impossible to be entirely objective and there is not a claim of any systematic difference.\n220\tConsider now a scene when Oppenheimer is walking down a dark street to an event and an FBI agent is taking notes of the license-plates of attendees.\n221\t\\ex\n222\t.\n223\tȮppenheimer sees a torch being shone at cars parked on a suburban street. [UK]\n224\t.̱ Oppenheimer notices someone with a flashlight checking out a car and taking a note.  [US]\n225\t.̧ Oppenheimer notices someone\n226\twith\n227\tshining\n228\ta\n229\tflashlight, checking out\n230\ttorch by\n231\ta car and\n232\ttaking a note.\n233\tmaking notes.\n234\t[post-edit]\n235\tThe British description employs the\n236\tpassive voice\n237\t, while the American description stays in the\n238\tactive voice\n239\t.\n240\tCampbell argues that in British AD the passive voice is used as a tool to direct the audience’s focus, while American AD typically avoids it.\n241\tThe guidelines do not suggest any major difference in syntactic construction between American and British AD.\n242\tConsider now how AD\n243\tnames characters\n244\t.\n245\tIn Oppenheimer, there are three board members in the hearing for his security clearance.\n246\tWe see the third board member, Ward Evans, at the back of the hearing from the beginning of the movie, but he is not named in the dialogue until towards the end of the movie (one of the other board members says ‘A full written opinion, with a dissent from Mr. Evans, will be issued to the AEC in the coming days.’).\n247\tA full 8 minutes before this utterance occurs in the movie, the British description opts to name him (‘A smile from Evans, the oldest board member.’).\n248\tIn contrast, at this point in the movie, the American description does not name him (‘An elderly member of the board smiles.’). In contrast again, in the post-edit, the name is introduced (‘An older member of the board, Ward Evans, smiles.’).\n249\tThe American version names him for the first time directly after the dialogue that introduces him (‘Robb shakes hands with the chairman, the elderly Evans and the third board member.’).\n250\tThe guidelines differ in whether they permit characters to be named before the sighted audience finds out their name: while the American guidelines stipulate that the description should always wait to name the character after the sighted audience finds out their name, British guidelines state that it is also acceptable to name a character earlier, unless there is mystery attached to the name.\n251\tCampbell argues that human describers use their own common sense to prioritise proportionate access to that of a sighted person.\n252\tThe creation of an AD script also involves selecting\n253\tsuitable moments to voice the description\n254\t.\n255\tThe British guidelines stipulate a strict rule to never voice description over dialogue.\n256\tMeanwhile, the American guidelines are somewhat more permissive.\n257\tAs one example, when Oppenheimer is left behind in a lab and his classmates leave, one of them shouts back “Don’t forget to clean up!”.\n258\tThe American version voices description over this entirely, while the British version does not.\n259\tThe American description opts to talk about a green apple which soon becomes salient to the narrative.\n260\tIn sum, claims of the differences between American and British audio description are as follows.\n261\t1.\n262\tAmerican terms\n263\t: American AD uses a higher frequency of American English lexical items.\n264\t2.\n265\tBritish terms\n266\t: British AD uses a higher frequency of British English lexical items.\n267\t3.\n268\tPresent progressive\n269\t: British AD uses the present progressive aspect more frequently.\n270\t4.\n271\tSubjectivity\n272\t: British AD exhibits greater subjectivity in interpreting visual information and character emotions.\n273\t5.\n274\tPassive\n275\t: British AD uses the passive voice more frequently.\n276\t6.\n277\tNaming in advance\n278\t: British AD names characters in advance more frequently.\n279\t7.\n280\tDialogue overlap\n281\t: American AD voices over dialogue (and music) more frequently.\n282\t3\n283\tMethods\n284\t3.1\n285\tMaterials\n286\tWe now test these claims using our pre-existing corpus of AD\n287\t[\n288\tsterner-2026-reframed\n289\t]\n290\t.\n291\tFor each of 206 movies, the corpus provides professional American and British AD, transcribed from their audio tracks with automatic methods.\n292\t4\n293\t4\n294\t4\n295\tThere is an automatic step that supports the correct spelling of character names. Example\n296\t1\n297\tis derived from the same automatic transcription, which resulted in the correct spellings of the characters.\n298\tAutomatic signal processing steps were taken to ensure that both tracks describe identical versions of the movie and to align them with each other.\n299\tFor every movie, the corpus also provides human-transcribed and automatically aligned dialogue subtitles.\n300\tThere also exists an accessibility service designed to cater to individuals who are deaf and hard of hearing, which is often called SDH (Subtitles for the Deaf and Hard-of-Hearing).\n301\tThese subtitles contain salient non-verbal audio (e.g., Lawrence exhaling during the Trinity explosion scene), unclear speaker identification (e.g., that Oppenheimer is narrating immediately after), and text simplification when dialogue is spoken too quickly to read (from a different movie, ‘I just thought that maybe this was gonna be more effective.’ becomes ‘I just thought this would be more effective.’).\n302\tAll 206 movies are also provided with these subtitles.\n303\tThe corpus additionally provides a number of video excerpts released publicly for the movies, as well as aligned screenplays.\n304\tAll automatic tools used in the construction of this dataset were validated against a separate set of ten full movies, for which we gathered professional transcription, manually annotated scene boundaries and manually annotated screenplay alignment.\n305\tScene boundary annotation was performed first based on the movie visuals, and then the British and American AD corresponding to each scene was determined.\n306\tFor our experiments, we will only use the corpus American AD, British AD, and the dialogue subtitles.\n307\t5\n308\t5\n309\t5\n310\tThe exception to this will be our analysis of scene changes, for which we will only use the ten manually processed movies.\n311\tMore details about the corpus and its construction are in\n312\t[\n313\tsterner-2026-reframed\n314\t]\n315\t.\n316\t3.2\n317\tAutomatic Pre-processing\n318\tWe process each AD and dialogue sentence with automatic NLP tools from the Stanza toolkit\n319\t[\n320\tqi-2020-stanza\n321\t]\n322\t.\n323\tThis provides syntactic parses in which the output features Universal Dependencies\n324\t[\n325\tmarneffe-2021-ud\n326\t]\n327\t, Penn Treebank POS tags\n328\t[\n329\tmarcus-1993-building\n330\t]\n331\tand named entity recognition according to\n332\t[\n333\tweischedel-2013-ontonotes\n334\t]\n335\t.\n336\tDetails on how we use the parsed output of the different versions of AD to estimate the frequency of the linguistic features of note are given below.\n337\t3.3\n338\tSignificance Testing\n339\tWe test differences between American and British AD for statistical significance with paired, two-tailed permutation tests, approximated by\n340\tN\n341\t=\n342\t10\n343\t,\n344\t000\n345\tN=10{,}000\n346\tand with threshold\n347\tα\n348\t=\n349\t0.05\n350\t\\alpha=0.05\n351\t.\n352\tThe intuition for this statistical test is that if there was not a systematic difference between American and British AD, we could randomly swap the ‘American’ and ‘British’ labels for any given movie AD without changing the average results.\n353\t3.4\n354\tStatistics\n355\tTable\n356\t1\n357\tgives some statistics of the AD and subtitle portions of the corpus.\n358\tSome differences between the British and American versions are already evident.\n359\tOn average, the AD of a single movie contains 588 and 831 sentences in the two versions, respectively.\n360\tWe observe significantly higher lexical diversity\n361\t6\n362\t6\n363\t6\n364\tWe used the length-controlled variant of type-token ratio from McKee\n365\t[\n366\tmckee-2000-measuring\n367\t]\n368\t.\n369\tand mean dependency distance in British AD, as compared to American AD.\n370\tLexical diversity is significantly lower than in the subtitles.\n371\tThe mean speech rate in both versions is higher than the 160 words per minute suggested in the American guidelines.\n372\tFinally, we observe that in AD, characters are mentioned significantly more than in dialogue (\n373\tp\n374\t<\n375\t0.01\n376\tp<0.01\n377\tfor AD variants).\n378\tThis aligns with the statement in the British guidelines that names tend to be used more frequently in AD than in normal speech.\n379\tTable 1:\n380\tCorpus statistics. The subtitles are not provided with sentence segmentation, nor do we rely on such segmentation for any analysis on the subtitles in this paper.\n381\t∗\n382\tThis number is artificially c. 4% lower for some of the movies, due to a step in our alignment that corrects the different speed at which movies are shown in the two regions.\n383\tUK\n384\tUS\n385\tSubtitles\n386\t# movies\n387\t206\n388\t206\n389\t206\n390\t206\n391\t206\n392\t206\n393\t# tokens\n394\t1 137 598\n395\t1\\,137\\,598\n396\t1 419 576\n397\t1\\,419\\,576\n398\t1 899 006\n399\t1\\,899\\,006\n400\t# sentences\n401\t121 123\n402\t121\\,123\n403\t171 224\n404\t171\\,224\n405\t# tokens per movie\n406\t5522\n407\t5522\n408\t±2064\n409\t6891\n410\t6891\n411\t±2323\n412\t9218\n413\t9218\n414\t±3397\n415\t# sentences per movie\n416\t588\n417\t588\n418\t±217\n419\t831\n420\t831\n421\t±237\n422\tLexical diversity\n423\t87.2\n424\t87.2\n425\t±10.1\n426\t84.5\n427\t84.5\n428\t±9.6\n429\t113.9\n430\t113.9\n431\t±12.5\n432\tMean dependency distance\n433\t2.5\n434\t2.5\n435\t±0.1\n436\t2.4\n437\t2.4\n438\t±0.1\n439\tSpeech rate (tokens per minute)\n440\t190.7\n441\t190.7\n442\t±13.1\n443\t∗\n444\t194.2\n445\t194.2\n446\t±12.5\n447\t167.0\n448\t167.0\n449\t±21.13\n450\tNumber of character names per movie\n451\t340.6\n452\t340.6\n453\t±136.1\n454\t448.8\n455\t448.8\n456\t±161.5\n457\t217.0\n458\t217.0\n459\t±101.7\n460\t3.5\n461\tExperimental Tests\n462\tLexical variation.\n463\tWe quantify lexical difference by measuring the frequency of American- and British-specific terminology.\n464\tLists of British and American terms are taken from online resources.\n465\t7\n466\t7\n467\t7\n468\tAmerican terms:\n469\thttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glossary_of_American_terms_not_widely_used_in_the_United_Kingdom\n470\t, British terms:\n471\thttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glossary_of_British_terms_not_widely_used_in_the_United_States\n472\t.\n473\tThere are 314 American terms and 677 British terms.\n474\tSome of these terms are only more frequent in one region for a particular sense, and our subsequent analysis will be sense-independent.\n475\tAs one step to address this, we remove from the American list words also in the top 5,000 most frequent words in the British National Corpus\n476\t[\n477\tleech-2001-bnc_frequencies\n478\t]\n479\t.\n480\tSimilarly, we remove from the British list the top 5,000 most frequent words in the Corpus of Contemporary American English\n481\t[\n482\tdavies-2008-coca\n483\t]\n484\t.\n485\tThis results in removing 3 and 14 words, respectively, leading to lists of length 311 and 663 words.\n486\tFurthermore, for each movie we exclude all terms matching or contained in character names in the movies (for instance in one movie, a character is called ‘ladybug’, which is removed from the American list for that movie).\n487\tWe compute the frequency of these terms per sentence within the AD transcripts and report a macro-average over movies.\n488\tAspect and voice.\n489\tFor differences in aspect, we identify instances of the present progressive by detecting when all of the following conditions are met in a sentence: (1) a gerund or present participle (2) has an auxiliary dependent form of ‘to be’ (3) which is in the present tense.\n490\tWe compute the proportion of such description sentences in each movie and report the macro-average.\n491\tWe identify sentences that use the passive voice by labelling sentences that contain any dependency relation marking a passive subject or passive auxiliary.\n492\tWe compute the proportion of description sentences containing passive constructions in each movie and report the macro-average.\n493\tSubjective judgments.\n494\tFor our automatic analysis of subjective judgments in AD, we focus on the use of adjectives and how they are modified.\n495\tDe Smedt\n496\t[\n497\tde-smedt-2012-vreselijk\n498\t]\n499\tannotates English adjectives with percentage subjectivity scores and provides an algorithm for incorporating scores from adverbial modifiers.\n500\tWe apply their approach and determine the subjectivity of each description sentence and report a macro-average over movies.\n501\tNaming in advance.\n502\tWe identify whether AD introduces a character’s name before they are named in the dialogue.\n503\tWe use character lists from IMDb (modulo uncredited characters and enumerated characters) and define a mapping based on all unique permuted variants of the name.\n504\tThe first named entity that maps to each character is then identified in each AD script and the dialogue subtitles.\n505\tWe keep occurrences that match both of the following conditions: (1) the character is identified as occurring in both AD versions earlier than 60 seconds after its occurrence in the dialogue subtitles, (2) at least one of the AD versions names the character between the time the character is named in the dialogue subtitles and 60 seconds later.\n506\tAfter these conditions, there are three cases: (1) neither AD version names in advance, (2) the American version names in advance, (3) the British version names in advance.\n507\tWe compute counts of cases (2)/(1)+(2)+(3) and (3)/(1)+(2)+(3) and report a macro average over the movies with at least one detected character matching the conditions (183/206 movies).\n508\tDialogue overlap and scene changes.\n509\tOur measure of dialogue overlap is the proportion of duration of AD that overlaps with the dialogue subtitles.\n510\tDialogue subtitles also include music lyrics.\n511\tFor our experiments on how scene changes are articulated, we only use the separate set of ten movies with manual scene annotation.\n512\tBased on this annotation, we train a BERT language model\n513\t[\n514\tdevlin-2019-bert\n515\t]\n516\tto identify scene boundaries based on the AD text alone.\n517\tThe model takes as input six sentences, and a separator token is placed between sentences three and four.\n518\tThe training task is binary classification, with labels of whether the separator token represents a scene boundary or not.\n519\tWe perform cross-validation on the ten movies, and report results based on training on the American, British or both AD versions.\n520\tFor fair comparisons, we need to control the amount of data available to each trained model.\n521\tEarlier we found that American AD is typically longer than British AD; training on both AD versions will always result in more data than training on only one.\n522\tWe address this by subsampling the data from each movie such that the number of positive and negative samples is equal to the number from the movie with the fewest samples.\n523\tThis equates to sampling 74 descriptions that start new scenes and 474 that do not.\n524\t4\n525\tResults and Discussion\n526\tTable 2:\n527\tResults based on a macro average over 206 movies (\n528\t∗\n529\tonly 183 movies; for the remainder, zero valid characters were detected for the analysis). In each row, the underlined result indicates the larger number of the two.\n530\tComparison\n531\tUnit\n532\tUK\n533\tUS\n534\tSignificance\n535\t(1)\n536\tAmerican terms\n537\t(terms per 1,000 sentences)\n538\t5.0\n539\t16.8\n540\tp\n541\t<\n542\t0.01\n543\tp<0.01\n544\t(2)\n545\tBritish terms\n546\t(terms per 1,000 sentences)\n547\t15.8\n548\t4.6\n549\tp\n550\t<\n551\t0.01\n552\tp<0.01\n553\t(3)\n554\tPresent progressive\n555\t(proportion of sentences, %)\n556\t3.0\n557\t0.3\n558\tp\n559\t<\n560\t0.01\n561\tp<0.01\n562\t(4)\n563\tSubjectivity\n564\t(mean sentence score, %)\n565\t16.2\n566\t13.3\n567\tp\n568\t<\n569\t0.01\n570\tp<0.01\n571\t(5)\n572\tPassive\n573\t(proportion of sentences, %)\n574\t4.6\n575\t1.3\n576\tp\n577\t<\n578\t0.01\n579\tp<0.01\n580\t(6)\n581\tNaming in advance\n582\t∗\n583\t(proportion of characters, %)\n584\t31.4\n585\t9.0\n586\tp\n587\t<\n588\t0.01\n589\tp<0.01\n590\t(7)\n591\tDialogue overlap\n592\t(proportion of AD time, %)\n593\t8.5\n594\t14.1\n595\tp\n596\t<\n597\t0.01\n598\tp<0.01\n599\tResults of the application of the tests to the corpus are given in Table\n600\t2\n601\t.\n602\tLexical variation.\n603\tRows 1 and 2 provide supporting evidence of a difference in lexicon.\n604\tThere are an average of 16.8 American terms per 1,000 sentences of American description, significantly more than the 5.0 in British description sentences.\n605\tThe British terms are used on average 15.8 times per 1,000 sentences of British description, significantly more than the 4.6 times in American description sentences.\n606\tThese frequencies are still low, in part because our lists are not comprehensive and in part also demonstrating that lexical variation only plays a small role in differences between the regions (around 1% of sentences).\n607\tThe most frequent American terms in American AD are ‘SUV’ (414 US counts vs. 134 UK counts; British equivalent might be ‘4x4’), ‘flashlight’ (337 US counts vs. 40 UK counts; ‘torch’), and ‘sidewalk’ (307 US counts vs. 19 UK counts; ‘pavement’).\n608\tThe most frequent British terms in British AD are ‘amongst’ (184 UK counts vs. 16 US; American equivalent might be ‘among’), ‘car park’ (152 UK counts vs. 2 US; ‘parking lot’), and ‘windscreen’ (138 UK counts vs. 6 US; ‘windshield’).\n609\tThese are unsurprising results, and a clear demonstration of why AD needs to be localised for these two dialects of English.\n610\tAspect and voice.\n611\tRow 3 provides clear evidence that the present progressive is rarely used in American description (0.3% of sentences) but used significantly more frequently in UK descriptions (3.0% of sentences).\n612\tPassive constructions are used more than three times as frequently in British AD compared to American AD (row 5; 4.6% vs. 1.3%,\n613\tp\n614\t<\n615\t0.01\n616\tp<0.01\n617\t).\n618\tEvidently these are both only tools in the toolkit of a British audio describer, albeit even then not frequently used tools.\n619\tBoth the progressive aspect and the passive voice are often considered to be more complex syntax than simple aspect and active voice; algorithms that are designed to simplify English often change passive sentences to active ones.\n620\tMeanwhile the American guidelines are more explicit than the British ones in their recommendation to use simple language — our results here can be seen as a consequence of this.\n621\tSubjective judgments.\n622\tBased on this analysis, we find a significantly higher average subjectivity score in British description sentences compared to American sentences (row 4; 16.2% vs. 13.3%,\n623\tp\n624\t<\n625\t0.01\n626\tp<0.01\n627\t).\n628\tFor instance, the word ‘beautiful’ (which has a score of 100% subjective) occurs almost three times more frequently in British description than in American description (87 vs. 35 occurrences).\n629\tIndeed the American guidelines directly address the use of this word, negatively stating that it is vague and evaluative.\n630\tConsider the following two uses of ‘beautiful’:\n631\t\\ex\n632\t. \n633\tĠlancing around the room, he turns back to find a beautiful woman glaring at him.  [UK]\n634\t.̱ He looks away, then looks back and finds a dark-haired young woman standing before him.  [US]\n635\t\\ex\n636\t. \n637\tḂridget notices the church is mainly filled with beautiful young women.  [UK]\n638\t.̱ Bridget notices many of the pews filled with fashionable young women.  [US]\n639\tIn example\n640\t4\n641\t, the British description opts for a more detailed description of how the character scans the room (‘Glancing around the room’ vs. ‘He looks away’), and recovers from this use of narration time by describing the woman as ‘beautiful’ (vs. ‘dark-haired’ and ‘young’).\n642\tIn example\n643\t4\n644\twe can see that the two versions are near-identical, except for the choice to use ‘beautiful’ (British description) as compared with the one syllable longer ‘fashionable’ (American description).\n645\tNaming in advance.\n646\tRow 6 gives evidence that in British AD characters are consistently named in advance more frequently (31.4% vs. 9.0% of characters identified in our analysis,\n647\tp\n648\t<\n649\t0.01\n650\tp<0.01\n651\t).\n652\tWhen a character is named in advance in British AD, they are named on average 11 minutes early, compared to seven minutes in American AD.\n653\tAt the extremes, on one occasion a character is named in British AD over 100 minutes before being named in the dialogue, and on another occasion a character is named over 60 minutes early in American AD.\n654\tBoth cases concern supporting characters that appear only briefly in the opening quarter of the movie but are more fully developed in the final quarter of the movie.\n655\tOur findings show that some American audio describers do opt to name in advance, even if this goes against their guidelines.\n656\tNote that the cases under consideration here only concern when one, and not both, of the versions opted to name in advance.\n657\tThe 9.0% of characters named in advance in American AD thus represents occasions when the British describer did not name in advance.\n658\tWe suspect that this number represents roughly how often describers from the same region will differ in their choices to name in advance.\n659\tThis leads to the suggestion that in 22.4% of cases the British describer made a regionality-influenced decision to name in advance.\n660\tIn most cases (59.6%), both versions are naming the character immediately after they are named in the dialogue.\n661\tDialogue overlap.\n662\tRow 7 shows that 14.1% of American description time overlaps with the subtitles, compared to the significantly lower 8.5% of British description time.\n663\tThis supports the claim that British AD is stricter about not overlapping with character dialogue and music.\n664\tThis result is, however, unsurprising given our earlier statistic that British AD uses on average 29.2% fewer sentences.\n665\tScene changes.\n666\tTable 3:\n667\tCross-validation results based on training a model to predict movie scene boundaries from AD transcripts.\n668\tUK\n669\tUS\n670\tP\n671\tP\n672\tR\n673\tR\n674\tF\n675\t1\n676\tF_{1}\n677\tP\n678\tP\n679\tR\n680\tR\n681\tF\n682\t1\n683\tF_{1}\n684\tTrained on UK\n685\t62.6\n686\t55.4\n687\t58.4\n688\t61.9\n689\t72.4\n690\t65.8\n691\tTrained on US\n692\t73.6\n693\t42.9\n694\t53.7\n695\t70.6\n696\t66.0\n697\t67.0\n698\tTrained on US+UK\n699\t66.1\n700\t55.1\n701\t59.6\n702\t65.1\n703\t69.0\n704\t65.9\n705\tResults are shown in Table\n706\t3\n707\t.\n708\tWe can see that recall is significantly lower for the task of identifying scene boundaries from British AD transcripts, as compared to from American AD transcripts (trained on UK: 55.4% vs. 72.4%,\n709\tp\n710\t=\n711\t0.003\n712\tp=0.003\n713\t; trained on US: 42.9% vs. 66.0%,\n714\tp\n715\t=\n716\t0.003\n717\tp=0.003\n718\t; trained on UK+US: 55.1% vs. 69.0%,\n719\tp\n720\t=\n721\t0.013\n722\tp=0.013\n723\t).\n724\tTraining only on American transcripts and testing on British transcripts leads to the worst performance (\n725\tF\n726\t1\n727\t=\n728\t53.7\n729\t%\n730\tF_{1}=53.7\\%\n731\t, differences to other\n732\tF\n733\t1\n734\tF_{1}\n735\tscores all have\n736\tp\n737\t≤\n738\t0.05\n739\tp\\leq 0.05\n740\t).\n741\tThis suggests that this model learnt to rely on cues (most likely explicit cues like ‘now’) in American transcripts that are not present as frequently in the British transcripts.\n742\tIn sum, these results provide supporting evidence that scene boundaries are verbalised less explicitly in British AD than in American AD.\n743\t5\n744\tConclusion\n745\tAD is a narrative technique.\n746\tWe have shown at scale the existence of small but statistically distinguishable differences between how the narrative is verbalised in American vs. British descriptions.\n747\tAll of the differences are justified based on differences between the regions’ guidelines, except for lexical variation, which has not received prior theoretical treatment.\n748\t1.\n749\tAmerican AD uses American terms roughly three times more frequently than British AD does; the same holds vice versa.\n750\t2.\n751\tAmerican AD rarely uses the progressive aspect or passive voice, while British AD uses these in 3% and 4% of sentences, respectively.\n752\t3.\n753\tBoth American and British AD use subjective adjectives and modifiers, but American AD uses them more sparsely.\n754\t4.\n755\tOf characters named in at least one version immediately after being named in the dialogue, American AD names that character in advance at one third of the frequency in British AD.\n756\t5.\n757\tBoth American and British AD, on occasion, narrate over dialogue, but American AD overlaps more frequently (and is also longer in general).\n758\tOur work contributes to a better understanding of the creation of AD.\n759\tIt can serve to help practitioners better analyse their own habits and encourage the field to make more active decisions in guidelines.\n760\tAD also serves as an attractive domain for research in narratology, because it is a highly constrained form of storytelling that makes the underlying processes of narrative construction in movies explicit.\n761\tUsing our corpus of British and American AD, we have here provided empirical evidence that AD is constrained both by dialect differences and by guidelines.\n762\tAcknowledgements\n763\tIS is funded by an EPSRC PhD studentship (project reference 2923920).\n764\tWe thank Nina Gregorio and Marie Campbell for comments.\n765\tReferences\n766\tMajor funding support from\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "92e8e122-1827-4162-a138-2994893b69bf", "uuid": "7e872ceb-9413-4fac-ac0b-d2d12c13ae01", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T11:20:15.174Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_full_text.txt", "content": "Comparing British and American Audio Description of Movies\nTitle:\nContent selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nDescription:\narXiv is now an independent nonprofit!\nLearn more\n×\nLicense: CC BY 4.0\narXiv:2608.09792v1 [cs.CL] 10 Aug 2026\n\\copyrightclause\nCopyright for this paper by its authors.\nUse permitted under Creative Commons License Attribution 4.0\nInternational (CC BY 4.0).\n\\conference\nCMN’26: Workshop on Computational Models of Narrative, June 8-10, 2026, Madrid, Spain.\n[email=igor.sterner@ed.ac.uk,\n]\n[email=alex@inf.ed.ac.uk,\n]\n[email=keller@inf.ed.ac.uk,\n]\nComparing British and American Audio Description of Movies\nIgor Sterner\nSchool of Informatics, University of Edinburgh, United Kingdom\nAlex Lascarides\nFrank Keller\n(2026)\nAbstract\nNarrating the visual component of movies is known as audio description. It is a narrative technique designed to enable blind and visually impaired individuals to follow the story. However, it is far more constrained than most narratives: the descriptions not only need to convey the story in the movie, but they must also fit into gaps between dialogue and they need to conform to guidelines that exist in each region. In this work, we compare audio description created in the United Kingdom against audio description created in the United States. We use guidelines written for these two regions, alongside the impressions from a practitioner in the field, to motivate specific hypotheses about the differences. We test these hypotheses against our pre-existing corpus, which provides both human-authored American and British audio description for each of 206 movies. Results provide quantitative evidence to uphold all tested hypotheses, including differences in lexicon, the use of the progressive aspect, the use of passive constructions, the use of subjective adjectives and modifiers, when characters are named, how scenes are cued, and degree of overlap with movie dialogue and music. Our work offers a quantitative lens into the narrative technique of audio description.\nkeywords:\nmovies\n\\sep\naudio description\n\\sep\nguidelines\n\\sep\nnarrative\n\\sep\ndialect\n1\nIntroduction\nMovies tell stories using a carefully curated mix of linguistic, auditory and visual information.\nAudiences extract meaning and narrative from all of this information, often to great enjoyment.\nHowever, being able to articulate that meaning-making process is a challenge.\nThis is a challenge tackled by accessibility services that cater to individuals for whom one or more modality is less available.\nThe accessibility service that aims to cater to blind and visually impaired individuals is called audio description (AD).\nAD is a translation of the visual (and unclear aural) information into a verbal narration.\nThe narration is constrained to fit in gaps between dialogue, and it is voiced by a neutral narrator.\nThe task requires an audio describer to begin with a full understanding of the narrative being told\n[\nvercauteren-2016-narratological\n]\n, which involves analysis of the full movie and potentially also a screenplay, dialogue subtitles, and the other available resources.\nThey must then identify suitable gaps between dialogue and find ways to translate the visual part of the narrative into a verbal narrative.\nEvery AD will be different, because these stages involve subjective interpretation and storytelling.\nAD is arguably a literary and narrative art form\n[\nsnyder-2008-audio\n]\n.\nThere is debate about how much creative license should be afforded to audio describers.\nThis debate spans a scale of strictly objective description of the visuals\n[\nadc_2009_guidelines\n]\nto a full re-narrativisation of the movie that prioritises coherent storytelling\n[\nkruger-2010-audio_narration\n]\n.\nFor practical purposes, some parts of this debate are answered for particular regions by their AD guidelines.\nFor instance, American guidelines\n[Annexe 4 of\nrai-2010-comparative\n]\nstipulate that all subjective interpretation is unacceptable, while British guidelines\n[Annexe 6 of\nrai-2010-comparative\n]\nstate that when it reduces potential confusion, particular visuals can be subject to a certain amount of interpretation.\nUltimately, however, all perception is interpretive and complete objectivity is simply impossible.\nAudio describers rely on their training, experience, and common sense in order to maximise the understanding and enjoyment (i.e., access) that they provide to their audience.\nGiven the overarching goal of providing AD is achieving access, the extent to which American and British AD scripts differ in practice has never been quantitatively evaluated.\nConsider the AD of the Trinity nuclear test scene in the 2023 movie Oppenheimer.\n1\n1\n1\nThe video of this scene with American AD is available here:\nhttps://adawardsgala.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/Oppenheimer.mp4\n.\n\\ex\n. \nK̇istiakowsky is open mouthed, staring through his goggles. In the car, Feynman screws up his eyes from the light. Oppenheimer takes off his goggles. An immense fireball billows into the night sky. Oppenheimer’s eyelids flicker open. The explosion climbs higher and higher, white heat at its core darkening at the edges into an orange mushroom shape. Lawrence is standing outside the car with his hand on the door, watching the spreading glow on the horizon. Oppenheimer stares into the blackened orange clouds with sparks flying in their midst. On his mattress, Rabi shifts onto his side for a clearer view, keeping the tinted glass in front of his face. Other observers sit up to watch the still-blossoming explosion. Rabi starts to lower his glass. Oppenheimer watches the light fade to a red glimmer.  [UK] \n.̱ Now, standing outside the car, Lawrence gapes as the bright line shines on his face, and Feynman squeezes his eyes shut. Teller’s lips puff out with a sharp exhale as his expression remains unchanged. In the bunker, Oppenheimer removes his goggles, blinking rapidly and squinting to see the explosion growing into a column of fire that blooms high into the dark sky, taking on a mushroom shape. Seen from over Lawrence’s shoulder, the explosion’s brilliant light gives off an amber halo around its irregular edges, which, miles away, tinges Oppenheimer’s awed face. The roiling flames billow over each other, black smoke starting to form within the inferno. Rabi finally rolls over and peers through his welder’s glass at the explosion. The amber glow on Oppenheimer’s face dims as the black smoke grows in the flames, which fold over on themselves. Rabi lowers the glass from his eyes as the blackness consumes the remaining flames, leaving behind a ghostly cloud of smoke in the dark.  [US]\nBoth the British and American versions (\n1\nand\n1\n, respectively) describe similar events in the scene, using language that is largely an objective description of the visuals.\nNevertheless, the language is almost poetic and paints a vivid scene.\nThere is some flexibility, especially in a short thread, as to when an action is described—e.g., compare the description of Lawrence outside a car watching the explosion (7th sentence in UK, 1st sentence in US).\nThere is also some flexibility on which of the less salient actions to describe, as we can see from the choices to describe only one of Kistiakowsky’s or Teller’s expressions.\nA part of the process of creating AD also relies on knowledge of who the audience will be.\nAD created in the United States will follow guidelines from that region; the same holds for AD created in other regions, in particular the United Kingdom.\nIn our study, we will only consider English AD from these two regions, since they form the largest available data, but English AD is also created for other regions (e.g., Australia) and for many other languages.\nEach region will have dialectal and lexical preferences, and audio describers naturally take this into account in their decisions on how to express content.\nComparing the differences between versions of AD can offer a lens into AD as a narrative technique.\nPrior work has relied on theoretical analyses of guidelines\n[\nbittner-2012-guidelines\n]\nand anecdotal evidence\n[\ncampbell-2025-crossing\n]\n.\nThey offer hypotheses as to the differences between American and British AD, but do not provide quantitative evidence in support of these hypotheses.\nIn this paper, we aim to address this gap.\nFor fair and controlled experiments, we need to compare AD of the same source material from each region.\nIn separate work, we have collected a corpus of AD transcripts that makes this possible; see\n[\nsterner-2026-reframed\n]\nfor more details on the corpus and on the automatic generation of AD.\nFor each of 206 movies, the corpus provides a professional American AD transcript and a professional British AD transcript.\nWe will begin by describing hypotheses about the differences between AD in the regions. These are based on lexical variation, aspect, voice, subjectivity, character naming, dialogue overlap and description of scene changes.\nThe specific hypotheses we define are motivated by the AD guidelines and claims made by a leading practitioner in the creation of AD.\nWe design and implement automatic methods that test these hypotheses, and apply them to our corpus.\nResults provide supporting evidence to uphold all of the hypotheses.\nWe finish by discussing the implications of our work.\n2\nHypotheses\nWe make hypotheses of the differences between American and British AD based on two sources.\nThe first source is the AD guidelines from each region.\n2\n2\n2\nRai\n[\nrai-2010-comparative\n]\nappends a snapshot of guidelines at the time of their writing to their appendix (the American guidelines we use are in Annexe 4 and British guidelines in Annexe 6).\nAD guidelines provide recommendations as to how audio describers should produce their descriptions.\nOur interpretation of the guidelines and the differences between them is based on Bittner’s analysis\n[\nbittner-2012-guidelines\n]\n.\nThe second source is Campbell’s claims in\n[\ncampbell-2025-crossing\n]\n.\nCampbell works in a leading role in the creation of AD at Red Bee Media, a large broadcasting and media services company.\nHer claims represent the perspective of an AD practitioner.\nFor this section, we will provide examples from one movie, Oppenheimer (2023).\nIts American AD was a finalist in both the EGA Hermes Awards and Audio Description Awards Gala.\nThe first unique aspect of this movie is that the officially released British AD is a post-edit of the American AD, which is uncommon (henceforth, we’ll refer to this version as ‘post-edit’).\n3\n3\n3\nThis is a presumption, based on observing the data and both stating that they are created by the same company, Deluxe. We presume that the American AD version of Oppenheimer was provided as a starting point to a team that scripted and voiced the British AD version.\nThe second unique aspect of this movie is that an entirely new UK AD version was created by the BBC for its broadcasting of the movie (henceforth, we’ll refer to this version as ‘UK’).\nConsider the following scene.\nOppenheimer has been in the New Mexico desert with Lawrence and Frank.\nOppenheimer says ‘Come’, and leads them off.\nWhen the\nscene changes\nto one later in the day in a tent, the AD versions are as follows.\n\\ex\n.\nİnside a tent, Lawrence drinks from a bottle and passes it to Frank.  [UK]\n.̱ After nightfall, warm lantern light glows on the men’s faces inside a tent. Lawrence passes a liquor bottle to Frank, who takes a swig.  [US]\n.̧\nAfter nightfall\nIn a tent at night\n, warm\nlantern\nlight glows on the men’s faces\ninside a tent\n. Lawrence passes a\nliquor\nbottle\nof booze\nto Frank, who takes a swig.  [post-edit]\nAll three versions cue the scene-change with a fronted adverbial (‘inside a tent’; ‘after nightfall’; ‘in a tent at night’).\nBoth British and American AD guidelines state that scene changes should be cued, in particular when it is difficult to infer from the soundtrack.\nThe American guidelines are more explicit in this recommendation, articulating that markers like ‘now’ can be used. In Oppenheimer, ‘now’ is used 16 times in the American version, almost all of which are removed in the post-edit (leaving 3), and also rarely used in the UK version (used 3 times).\nIn contrast, the British guidelines do not provide any explicit suggestions as to how signalling a change in scene should be achieved.\nWe can also see\nlexical variation\nin how the alcoholic beverage is described (UK and post-edit: ‘a bottle’ or ‘a bottle of booze’; US: ‘liquor bottle’).\nThis difference is raised by Campbell, but not by any of the region-specific guidelines.\nConsider now the following description, which occurs at the end of a scene when Oppenheimer is giving a lecture in Holland.\n\\ex\n.\nȮppenheimer is standing at the lectern.  [UK]\n.̱ Oppenheimer stands at a lectern.  [US]\n.̧\nAt the front,\nOppenheimer stands at a lectern\nlooking slightly nervous\n.  [post-edit]\nThe British and American descriptions are near-identical, except for the fact that they differ in\naspect\n.\nBoth are in the present tense, but the British description employs the progressive aspect, while the American description is simple.\nCampbell claims that American descriptions employ almost exclusively the simple present tense, while in British descriptions there is the option to switch to the present progressive and simple present for variation.\nThis claim is supported by the guidelines, which all state that the present tense should be used, with the guidelines from the UK and from Ireland additionally allowing progressive aspect to be used.\nIn the post-edited version of the American description, the describer has also chosen to incorporate a more detailed description of Oppenheimer’s body language (‘looking slightly nervous’).\nNote that the describer has chosen not to describe the visuals directly (e.g. Oppenheimer’s pursed lips or avoidant eyes).\nInstead the adjective ‘nervous’, additionally modified by the adverb ‘slightly’, is used to make a judgment about Oppenheimer’s emotions.\nIn this case, this decision was presumably made due to the very short gap between dialogue into which this description has to fit.\nThe British guidelines explicitly afford flexibility with regard to\nsubjective interpretation\n, in particular when this reduces potential confusion.\nHowever, the American guidelines are explicit in discouraging this.\nCampbell acknowledges the assumption that British AD tends to do more work in interpreting emotions than American AD.\nHowever, she argues that it is impossible to be entirely objective and there is not a claim of any systematic difference.\nConsider now a scene when Oppenheimer is walking down a dark street to an event and an FBI agent is taking notes of the license-plates of attendees.\n\\ex\n.\nȮppenheimer sees a torch being shone at cars parked on a suburban street. [UK]\n.̱ Oppenheimer notices someone with a flashlight checking out a car and taking a note.  [US]\n.̧ Oppenheimer notices someone\nwith\nshining\na\nflashlight, checking out\ntorch by\na car and\ntaking a note.\nmaking notes.\n[post-edit]\nThe British description employs the\npassive voice\n, while the American description stays in the\nactive voice\n.\nCampbell argues that in British AD the passive voice is used as a tool to direct the audience’s focus, while American AD typically avoids it.\nThe guidelines do not suggest any major difference in syntactic construction between American and British AD.\nConsider now how AD\nnames characters\n.\nIn Oppenheimer, there are three board members in the hearing for his security clearance.\nWe see the third board member, Ward Evans, at the back of the hearing from the beginning of the movie, but he is not named in the dialogue until towards the end of the movie (one of the other board members says ‘A full written opinion, with a dissent from Mr. Evans, will be issued to the AEC in the coming days.’).\nA full 8 minutes before this utterance occurs in the movie, the British description opts to name him (‘A smile from Evans, the oldest board member.’).\nIn contrast, at this point in the movie, the American description does not name him (‘An elderly member of the board smiles.’). In contrast again, in the post-edit, the name is introduced (‘An older member of the board, Ward Evans, smiles.’).\nThe American version names him for the first time directly after the dialogue that introduces him (‘Robb shakes hands with the chairman, the elderly Evans and the third board member.’).\nThe guidelines differ in whether they permit characters to be named before the sighted audience finds out their name: while the American guidelines stipulate that the description should always wait to name the character after the sighted audience finds out their name, British guidelines state that it is also acceptable to name a character earlier, unless there is mystery attached to the name.\nCampbell argues that human describers use their own common sense to prioritise proportionate access to that of a sighted person.\nThe creation of an AD script also involves selecting\nsuitable moments to voice the description\n.\nThe British guidelines stipulate a strict rule to never voice description over dialogue.\nMeanwhile, the American guidelines are somewhat more permissive.\nAs one example, when Oppenheimer is left behind in a lab and his classmates leave, one of them shouts back “Don’t forget to clean up!”.\nThe American version voices description over this entirely, while the British version does not.\nThe American description opts to talk about a green apple which soon becomes salient to the narrative.\nIn sum, claims of the differences between American and British audio description are as follows.\n1.\nAmerican terms\n: American AD uses a higher frequency of American English lexical items.\n2.\nBritish terms\n: British AD uses a higher frequency of British English lexical items.\n3.\nPresent progressive\n: British AD uses the present progressive aspect more frequently.\n4.\nSubjectivity\n: British AD exhibits greater subjectivity in interpreting visual information and character emotions.\n5.\nPassive\n: British AD uses the passive voice more frequently.\n6.\nNaming in advance\n: British AD names characters in advance more frequently.\n7.\nDialogue overlap\n: American AD voices over dialogue (and music) more frequently.\n3\nMethods\n3.1\nMaterials\nWe now test these claims using our pre-existing corpus of AD\n[\nsterner-2026-reframed\n]\n.\nFor each of 206 movies, the corpus provides professional American and British AD, transcribed from their audio tracks with automatic methods.\n4\n4\n4\nThere is an automatic step that supports the correct spelling of character names. Example\n1\nis derived from the same automatic transcription, which resulted in the correct spellings of the characters.\nAutomatic signal processing steps were taken to ensure that both tracks describe identical versions of the movie and to align them with each other.\nFor every movie, the corpus also provides human-transcribed and automatically aligned dialogue subtitles.\nThere also exists an accessibility service designed to cater to individuals who are deaf and hard of hearing, which is often called SDH (Subtitles for the Deaf and Hard-of-Hearing).\nThese subtitles contain salient non-verbal audio (e.g., Lawrence exhaling during the Trinity explosion scene), unclear speaker identification (e.g., that Oppenheimer is narrating immediately after), and text simplification when dialogue is spoken too quickly to read (from a different movie, ‘I just thought that maybe this was gonna be more effective.’ becomes ‘I just thought this would be more effective.’).\nAll 206 movies are also provided with these subtitles.\nThe corpus additionally provides a number of video excerpts released publicly for the movies, as well as aligned screenplays.\nAll automatic tools used in the construction of this dataset were validated against a separate set of ten full movies, for which we gathered professional transcription, manually annotated scene boundaries and manually annotated screenplay alignment.\nScene boundary annotation was performed first based on the movie visuals, and then the British and American AD corresponding to each scene was determined.\nFor our experiments, we will only use the corpus American AD, British AD, and the dialogue subtitles.\n5\n5\n5\nThe exception to this will be our analysis of scene changes, for which we will only use the ten manually processed movies.\nMore details about the corpus and its construction are in\n[\nsterner-2026-reframed\n]\n.\n3.2\nAutomatic Pre-processing\nWe process each AD and dialogue sentence with automatic NLP tools from the Stanza toolkit\n[\nqi-2020-stanza\n]\n.\nThis provides syntactic parses in which the output features Universal Dependencies\n[\nmarneffe-2021-ud\n]\n, Penn Treebank POS tags\n[\nmarcus-1993-building\n]\nand named entity recognition according to\n[\nweischedel-2013-ontonotes\n]\n.\nDetails on how we use the parsed output of the different versions of AD to estimate the frequency of the linguistic features of note are given below.\n3.3\nSignificance Testing\nWe test differences between American and British AD for statistical significance with paired, two-tailed permutation tests, approximated by\nN\n=\n10\n,\n000\nN=10{,}000\nand with threshold\nα\n=\n0.05\n\\alpha=0.05\n.\nThe intuition for this statistical test is that if there was not a systematic difference between American and British AD, we could randomly swap the ‘American’ and ‘British’ labels for any given movie AD without changing the average results.\n3.4\nStatistics\nTable\n1\ngives some statistics of the AD and subtitle portions of the corpus.\nSome differences between the British and American versions are already evident.\nOn average, the AD of a single movie contains 588 and 831 sentences in the two versions, respectively.\nWe observe significantly higher lexical diversity\n6\n6\n6\nWe used the length-controlled variant of type-token ratio from McKee\n[\nmckee-2000-measuring\n]\n.\nand mean dependency distance in British AD, as compared to American AD.\nLexical diversity is significantly lower than in the subtitles.\nThe mean speech rate in both versions is higher than the 160 words per minute suggested in the American guidelines.\nFinally, we observe that in AD, characters are mentioned significantly more than in dialogue (\np\n<\n0.01\np<0.01\nfor AD variants).\nThis aligns with the statement in the British guidelines that names tend to be used more frequently in AD than in normal speech.\nTable 1:\nCorpus statistics. The subtitles are not provided with sentence segmentation, nor do we rely on such segmentation for any analysis on the subtitles in this paper.\n∗\nThis number is artificially c. 4% lower for some of the movies, due to a step in our alignment that corrects the different speed at which movies are shown in the two regions.\nUK\nUS\nSubtitles\n# movies\n206\n206\n206\n206\n206\n206\n# tokens\n1 137 598\n1\\,137\\,598\n1 419 576\n1\\,419\\,576\n1 899 006\n1\\,899\\,006\n# sentences\n121 123\n121\\,123\n171 224\n171\\,224\n# tokens per movie\n5522\n5522\n±2064\n6891\n6891\n±2323\n9218\n9218\n±3397\n# sentences per movie\n588\n588\n±217\n831\n831\n±237\nLexical diversity\n87.2\n87.2\n±10.1\n84.5\n84.5\n±9.6\n113.9\n113.9\n±12.5\nMean dependency distance\n2.5\n2.5\n±0.1\n2.4\n2.4\n±0.1\nSpeech rate (tokens per minute)\n190.7\n190.7\n±13.1\n∗\n194.2\n194.2\n±12.5\n167.0\n167.0\n±21.13\nNumber of character names per movie\n340.6\n340.6\n±136.1\n448.8\n448.8\n±161.5\n217.0\n217.0\n±101.7\n3.5\nExperimental Tests\nLexical variation.\nWe quantify lexical difference by measuring the frequency of American- and British-specific terminology.\nLists of British and American terms are taken from online resources.\n7\n7\n7\nAmerican terms:\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glossary_of_American_terms_not_widely_used_in_the_United_Kingdom\n, British terms:\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glossary_of_British_terms_not_widely_used_in_the_United_States\n.\nThere are 314 American terms and 677 British terms.\nSome of these terms are only more frequent in one region for a particular sense, and our subsequent analysis will be sense-independent.\nAs one step to address this, we remove from the American list words also in the top 5,000 most frequent words in the British National Corpus\n[\nleech-2001-bnc_frequencies\n]\n.\nSimilarly, we remove from the British list the top 5,000 most frequent words in the Corpus of Contemporary American English\n[\ndavies-2008-coca\n]\n.\nThis results in removing 3 and 14 words, respectively, leading to lists of length 311 and 663 words.\nFurthermore, for each movie we exclude all terms matching or contained in character names in the movies (for instance in one movie, a character is called ‘ladybug’, which is removed from the American list for that movie).\nWe compute the frequency of these terms per sentence within the AD transcripts and report a macro-average over movies.\nAspect and voice.\nFor differences in aspect, we identify instances of the present progressive by detecting when all of the following conditions are met in a sentence: (1) a gerund or present participle (2) has an auxiliary dependent form of ‘to be’ (3) which is in the present tense.\nWe compute the proportion of such description sentences in each movie and report the macro-average.\nWe identify sentences that use the passive voice by labelling sentences that contain any dependency relation marking a passive subject or passive auxiliary.\nWe compute the proportion of description sentences containing passive constructions in each movie and report the macro-average.\nSubjective judgments.\nFor our automatic analysis of subjective judgments in AD, we focus on the use of adjectives and how they are modified.\nDe Smedt\n[\nde-smedt-2012-vreselijk\n]\nannotates English adjectives with percentage subjectivity scores and provides an algorithm for incorporating scores from adverbial modifiers.\nWe apply their approach and determine the subjectivity of each description sentence and report a macro-average over movies.\nNaming in advance.\nWe identify whether AD introduces a character’s name before they are named in the dialogue.\nWe use character lists from IMDb (modulo uncredited characters and enumerated characters) and define a mapping based on all unique permuted variants of the name.\nThe first named entity that maps to each character is then identified in each AD script and the dialogue subtitles.\nWe keep occurrences that match both of the following conditions: (1) the character is identified as occurring in both AD versions earlier than 60 seconds after its occurrence in the dialogue subtitles, (2) at least one of the AD versions names the character between the time the character is named in the dialogue subtitles and 60 seconds later.\nAfter these conditions, there are three cases: (1) neither AD version names in advance, (2) the American version names in advance, (3) the British version names in advance.\nWe compute counts of cases (2)/(1)+(2)+(3) and (3)/(1)+(2)+(3) and report a macro average over the movies with at least one detected character matching the conditions (183/206 movies).\nDialogue overlap and scene changes.\nOur measure of dialogue overlap is the proportion of duration of AD that overlaps with the dialogue subtitles.\nDialogue subtitles also include music lyrics.\nFor our experiments on how scene changes are articulated, we only use the separate set of ten movies with manual scene annotation.\nBased on this annotation, we train a BERT language model\n[\ndevlin-2019-bert\n]\nto identify scene boundaries based on the AD text alone.\nThe model takes as input six sentences, and a separator token is placed between sentences three and four.\nThe training task is binary classification, with labels of whether the separator token represents a scene boundary or not.\nWe perform cross-validation on the ten movies, and report results based on training on the American, British or both AD versions.\nFor fair comparisons, we need to control the amount of data available to each trained model.\nEarlier we found that American AD is typically longer than British AD; training on both AD versions will always result in more data than training on only one.\nWe address this by subsampling the data from each movie such that the number of positive and negative samples is equal to the number from the movie with the fewest samples.\nThis equates to sampling 74 descriptions that start new scenes and 474 that do not.\n4\nResults and Discussion\nTable 2:\nResults based on a macro average over 206 movies (\n∗\nonly 183 movies; for the remainder, zero valid characters were detected for the analysis). In each row, the underlined result indicates the larger number of the two.\nComparison\nUnit\nUK\nUS\nSignificance\n(1)\nAmerican terms\n(terms per 1,000 sentences)\n5.0\n16.8\np\n<\n0.01\np<0.01\n(2)\nBritish terms\n(terms per 1,000 sentences)\n15.8\n4.6\np\n<\n0.01\np<0.01\n(3)\nPresent progressive\n(proportion of sentences, %)\n3.0\n0.3\np\n<\n0.01\np<0.01\n(4)\nSubjectivity\n(mean sentence score, %)\n16.2\n13.3\np\n<\n0.01\np<0.01\n(5)\nPassive\n(proportion of sentences, %)\n4.6\n1.3\np\n<\n0.01\np<0.01\n(6)\nNaming in advance\n∗\n(proportion of characters, %)\n31.4\n9.0\np\n<\n0.01\np<0.01\n(7)\nDialogue overlap\n(proportion of AD time, %)\n8.5\n14.1\np\n<\n0.01\np<0.01\nResults of the application of the tests to the corpus are given in Table\n2\n.\nLexical variation.\nRows 1 and 2 provide supporting evidence of a difference in lexicon.\nThere are an average of 16.8 American terms per 1,000 sentences of American description, significantly more than the 5.0 in British description sentences.\nThe British terms are used on average 15.8 times per 1,000 sentences of British description, significantly more than the 4.6 times in American description sentences.\nThese frequencies are still low, in part because our lists are not comprehensive and in part also demonstrating that lexical variation only plays a small role in differences between the regions (around 1% of sentences).\nThe most frequent American terms in American AD are ‘SUV’ (414 US counts vs. 134 UK counts; British equivalent might be ‘4x4’), ‘flashlight’ (337 US counts vs. 40 UK counts; ‘torch’), and ‘sidewalk’ (307 US counts vs. 19 UK counts; ‘pavement’).\nThe most frequent British terms in British AD are ‘amongst’ (184 UK counts vs. 16 US; American equivalent might be ‘among’), ‘car park’ (152 UK counts vs. 2 US; ‘parking lot’), and ‘windscreen’ (138 UK counts vs. 6 US; ‘windshield’).\nThese are unsurprising results, and a clear demonstration of why AD needs to be localised for these two dialects of English.\nAspect and voice.\nRow 3 provides clear evidence that the present progressive is rarely used in American description (0.3% of sentences) but used significantly more frequently in UK descriptions (3.0% of sentences).\nPassive constructions are used more than three times as frequently in British AD compared to American AD (row 5; 4.6% vs. 1.3%,\np\n<\n0.01\np<0.01\n).\nEvidently these are both only tools in the toolkit of a British audio describer, albeit even then not frequently used tools.\nBoth the progressive aspect and the passive voice are often considered to be more complex syntax than simple aspect and active voice; algorithms that are designed to simplify English often change passive sentences to active ones.\nMeanwhile the American guidelines are more explicit than the British ones in their recommendation to use simple language — our results here can be seen as a consequence of this.\nSubjective judgments.\nBased on this analysis, we find a significantly higher average subjectivity score in British description sentences compared to American sentences (row 4; 16.2% vs. 13.3%,\np\n<\n0.01\np<0.01\n).\nFor instance, the word ‘beautiful’ (which has a score of 100% subjective) occurs almost three times more frequently in British description than in American description (87 vs. 35 occurrences).\nIndeed the American guidelines directly address the use of this word, negatively stating that it is vague and evaluative.\nConsider the following two uses of ‘beautiful’:\n\\ex\n. \nĠlancing around the room, he turns back to find a beautiful woman glaring at him.  [UK]\n.̱ He looks away, then looks back and finds a dark-haired young woman standing before him.  [US]\n\\ex\n. \nḂridget notices the church is mainly filled with beautiful young women.  [UK]\n.̱ Bridget notices many of the pews filled with fashionable young women.  [US]\nIn example\n4\n, the British description opts for a more detailed description of how the character scans the room (‘Glancing around the room’ vs. ‘He looks away’), and recovers from this use of narration time by describing the woman as ‘beautiful’ (vs. ‘dark-haired’ and ‘young’).\nIn example\n4\nwe can see that the two versions are near-identical, except for the choice to use ‘beautiful’ (British description) as compared with the one syllable longer ‘fashionable’ (American description).\nNaming in advance.\nRow 6 gives evidence that in British AD characters are consistently named in advance more frequently (31.4% vs. 9.0% of characters identified in our analysis,\np\n<\n0.01\np<0.01\n).\nWhen a character is named in advance in British AD, they are named on average 11 minutes early, compared to seven minutes in American AD.\nAt the extremes, on one occasion a character is named in British AD over 100 minutes before being named in the dialogue, and on another occasion a character is named over 60 minutes early in American AD.\nBoth cases concern supporting characters that appear only briefly in the opening quarter of the movie but are more fully developed in the final quarter of the movie.\nOur findings show that some American audio describers do opt to name in advance, even if this goes against their guidelines.\nNote that the cases under consideration here only concern when one, and not both, of the versions opted to name in advance.\nThe 9.0% of characters named in advance in American AD thus represents occasions when the British describer did not name in advance.\nWe suspect that this number represents roughly how often describers from the same region will differ in their choices to name in advance.\nThis leads to the suggestion that in 22.4% of cases the British describer made a regionality-influenced decision to name in advance.\nIn most cases (59.6%), both versions are naming the character immediately after they are named in the dialogue.\nDialogue overlap.\nRow 7 shows that 14.1% of American description time overlaps with the subtitles, compared to the significantly lower 8.5% of British description time.\nThis supports the claim that British AD is stricter about not overlapping with character dialogue and music.\nThis result is, however, unsurprising given our earlier statistic that British AD uses on average 29.2% fewer sentences.\nScene changes.\nTable 3:\nCross-validation results based on training a model to predict movie scene boundaries from AD transcripts.\nUK\nUS\nP\nP\nR\nR\nF\n1\nF_{1}\nP\nP\nR\nR\nF\n1\nF_{1}\nTrained on UK\n62.6\n55.4\n58.4\n61.9\n72.4\n65.8\nTrained on US\n73.6\n42.9\n53.7\n70.6\n66.0\n67.0\nTrained on US+UK\n66.1\n55.1\n59.6\n65.1\n69.0\n65.9\nResults are shown in Table\n3\n.\nWe can see that recall is significantly lower for the task of identifying scene boundaries from British AD transcripts, as compared to from American AD transcripts (trained on UK: 55.4% vs. 72.4%,\np\n=\n0.003\np=0.003\n; trained on US: 42.9% vs. 66.0%,\np\n=\n0.003\np=0.003\n; trained on UK+US: 55.1% vs. 69.0%,\np\n=\n0.013\np=0.013\n).\nTraining only on American transcripts and testing on British transcripts leads to the worst performance (\nF\n1\n=\n53.7\n%\nF_{1}=53.7\\%\n, differences to other\nF\n1\nF_{1}\nscores all have\np\n≤\n0.05\np\\leq 0.05\n).\nThis suggests that this model learnt to rely on cues (most likely explicit cues like ‘now’) in American transcripts that are not present as frequently in the British transcripts.\nIn sum, these results provide supporting evidence that scene boundaries are verbalised less explicitly in British AD than in American AD.\n5\nConclusion\nAD is a narrative technique.\nWe have shown at scale the existence of small but statistically distinguishable differences between how the narrative is verbalised in American vs. British descriptions.\nAll of the differences are justified based on differences between the regions’ guidelines, except for lexical variation, which has not received prior theoretical treatment.\n1.\nAmerican AD uses American terms roughly three times more frequently than British AD does; the same holds vice versa.\n2.\nAmerican AD rarely uses the progressive aspect or passive voice, while British AD uses these in 3% and 4% of sentences, respectively.\n3.\nBoth American and British AD use subjective adjectives and modifiers, but American AD uses them more sparsely.\n4.\nOf characters named in at least one version immediately after being named in the dialogue, American AD names that character in advance at one third of the frequency in British AD.\n5.\nBoth American and British AD, on occasion, narrate over dialogue, but American AD overlaps more frequently (and is also longer in general).\nOur work contributes to a better understanding of the creation of AD.\nIt can serve to help practitioners better analyse their own habits and encourage the field to make more active decisions in guidelines.\nAD also serves as an attractive domain for research in narratology, because it is a highly constrained form of storytelling that makes the underlying processes of narrative construction in movies explicit.\nUsing our corpus of British and American AD, we have here provided empirical evidence that AD is constrained both by dialect differences and by guidelines.\nAcknowledgements\nIS is funded by an EPSRC PhD studentship (project reference 2923920).\nWe thank Nina Gregorio and Marie Campbell for comments.\nReferences\nMajor funding support from", "numLines": 766, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 766}}}
